WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.274
When America voted in 2020, 

00:00:02.274 --> 00:00:06.235
there was one issue both Republicans
and Democrats could agree on:

00:00:06.235 --> 00:00:10.200
"An overwhelming majority of voters said yes
to the legalization of marijuana."

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.900
"Montana voters gave their
clear backing to marijuana."

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.311
"Arizona, South Dakota..."

00:00:14.311 --> 00:00:18.390
"You could say a lot of New Jersey voters
are high tonight."

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.121
New Jersey arrests around 30,000 people a
year for marijuana possession,

00:00:23.121 --> 00:00:25.740
more than almost any other state.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.900
But this year they voted to legalize marijuana.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.360
Arizona voted to legalize it, too.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.759
So did Montana.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.653
So did South Dakota.

00:00:34.653 --> 00:00:38.272
Medical marijuana was passed in Mississippi.

00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:43.885
Now one in three Americans live in a state
where access to marijuana has been legalized.

00:00:43.885 --> 00:00:50.111
Oregon took it even further and decriminalized
possession of all drugs on Election Day.

00:00:50.111 --> 00:00:55.240
Over decades, America’s war on drugs has
put millions of people in prison.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.530
And today it’s widely understood to have
disproportionately affected people of color.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:05.016
For example, Black Americans use marijuana
at the same rates as White Americans,

00:01:05.016 --> 00:01:08.479
but are arrested for it at a much higher rate.

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:11.952
This map shows that more
and more Americans are starting to turn

00:01:11.952 --> 00:01:14.670
against the country’s harsh drug laws.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.806
But ending them entirely
will be a lot more complicated.

00:01:23.084 --> 00:01:27.670
Americans used to be almost unanimously
against legalizing marijuana.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.159
Today, two out of
three Americans support it.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:33.542
But politicians, not so much.

00:01:33.542 --> 00:01:38.620
There's a lag, between public embrace of issues,
particularly cannabis policy,

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:42.570
and state legislators,
or even members of Congress.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:47.460
John Hudak is a policy researcher who writes
about America’s marijuana policies.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.563
And he says the gap between how politicians
and the public feel about marijuana, 

00:01:51.563 --> 00:01:55.451
has a lot to do with
what kind of political issue it is:

00:01:55.451 --> 00:01:57.299
Most Americans don't use cannabis.

00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:02.079
Most Americans have never been arrested for a
cannabis-related offense, etc.

00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:04.343
So it ends up not being something

00:02:04.343 --> 00:02:09.024
that they are going to hold
elected officials accountable for.

00:02:09.024 --> 00:02:11.545
Americans usually choose who they vote for

00:02:11.545 --> 00:02:14.500
because of issues like
the economy, or health care.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.737
Issues like marijuana are pretty far down
the list of priorities.

00:02:18.737 --> 00:02:23.625
But when you ask them directly, Hey,
do you want marijuana legalized? They'll say yes.

00:02:23.625 --> 00:02:28.668
And that’s why almost all of these states
have legalized marijuana in a very similar way:

00:02:28.668 --> 00:02:32.989
Instead of the state legislature passing a
comprehensive, detailed law,

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:37.597
it was put directly to the people on Election
Day, as a question on the ballot.

00:02:37.597 --> 00:02:44.909
We have used ballot initiatives as a campaign
and advocacy tool for decades.

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:47.682
Lindsay LaSalle is a drug policy strategist,

00:02:47.682 --> 00:02:50.638
and she’s worked on a lot of these
state ballot initiatives.

00:02:50.638 --> 00:02:53.730
The legislature is often afraid to act.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.840
But one problem with changing laws this way,
with a simple ballot initiative,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.970
is that the state still has to figure out the details.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.049
And that isn’t always easy.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:06.471
For example, in New Jersey, no one really
knows what’s going to happen

00:03:06.471 --> 00:03:10.135
to all the people who are incarcerated,
or have arrest records,

00:03:10.135 --> 00:03:13.006
for something that's now legal.

00:03:13.006 --> 00:03:17.046
The other challenge for these laws is that
they create a gap with the federal government.

00:03:17.046 --> 00:03:21.810
Even though it’s legal in several states,
at the federal level marijuana is still classified

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:27.000
as one of the most serious drugs —
equal to heroin and LSD.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.350
And that puts federal drug laws in direct
conflict with state laws in all sorts of ways.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:37.093
Legal marijuana businesses have a really hard
time getting any federally-backed bank

00:03:37.093 --> 00:03:38.538
to take their money.

00:03:38.538 --> 00:03:41.279
And they can’t sell their product
across state lines.

00:03:41.279 --> 00:03:45.338
So a farm in California
can’t sell to a store in Nevada,

00:03:45.338 --> 00:03:48.219
even though it’s legal in both states.

00:03:48.219 --> 00:03:52.319
And because the federal government considers
marijuana a controlled substance,

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:57.492
scientists researching the effects of it often
face problems with funding and testing.

00:03:57.492 --> 00:04:01.540
But as more people in more states choose to
legalize marijuana,

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.289
this gap is going to become unsustainable.

00:04:04.289 --> 00:04:10.870
Having the more conservative states, like South
Dakota and Montana and Arizona, passing it,

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:16.747
means that people have to consider it at the
federal level in a much more robust way.

00:04:16.747 --> 00:04:19.353
We’ve seen something
similar happen before.

00:04:19.353 --> 00:04:24.786
At first, same-sex marriage only became legal
in the US state by state.

00:04:24.786 --> 00:04:31.808
But by 2015, 70% of Americans lived in states
that had legal same-sex marriage.

00:04:31.808 --> 00:04:35.668
That same year, it became legal
throughout the country.

00:04:35.668 --> 00:04:40.449
We've transformed, in this country,
in the course of about 25 to 30 years,

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:46.206
in terms of cannabis legalization being
an absolutely toxic and fringe issue,

00:04:46.206 --> 00:04:47.922
which is what it was,

00:04:47.922 --> 00:04:53.027
to one now where candidates
of both parties are embracing it.

00:04:53.027 --> 00:04:58.746
Americans' attitudes on the war on drugs,
and the mass incarceration it led to, are changing. 

00:04:58.746 --> 00:05:02.480
And more and more of them
are ready for those laws to change.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.546
But if politicians won’t do it,
they will.

